.TH cumonitor n 0.2 ctrlutils
.SH ctrlutils::cumonitor
.PP
A controller that watches one directory through the provider interface and fires a callback when its contents change. It \fBpolls\fR rather than using an OS watch (inotify), because polling is the only mechanism that works the same over a local filesystem, a ZIP, or a remote provider.
.SS API
.CS
package require ctrlutils::cumonitor

::ctrlutils::cumonitor::install $f $prov ?-interval ms? ?-onchange script?
::ctrlutils::cumonitor::watch   $f $dir
::ctrlutils::cumonitor::poke    $f
::ctrlutils::cumonitor::stop    $f
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBinstall\fR \-\- set up a monitor for the view \fB$f\fR over \fB$prov\fR. \fB\-interval\fR is the poll period in ms (default 2000); \fB\-onchange script\fR is appended the changed directory. An unknown option is an error listing the known ones, \fB{CTRLUTILS CUMONITOR OPTION <opt>}\fR (0.2; before, a plain error without errorcode).
.IP \(bu
\fBwatch $f $dir\fR \-\- watch \fB$dir\fR (re\-baselines silently on each call, so navigating between directories does not look like a change).
.IP \(bu
\fBpoke $f\fR \-\- absorb the current state as the new baseline, without firing. Call it after the application's own edits so the monitor does not report them back.
.IP \(bu
\fBstop $f\fR \-\- stop watching and drop the state (idempotent).
.SS "How it detects change"
.PP
Each poll reads the directory via the provider's \fBlist\fR and diffs a signature of the entries (name, type, size, mtime). The first snapshot of a directory is the baseline and does not fire; only a real difference from it fires. Because it goes through the provider, it is storage\-agnostic \-\- the same watcher works over any backend.
.SS "Typical wiring"
.CS
::ctrlutils::cumonitor::install $f $prov -interval 2000 \
    -onchange {apply {d {reloadList $d}}}
::ctrlutils::cumonitor::watch $f $currentDir
# after the app changes files itself:
::ctrlutils::cumonitor::poke $f
.CE
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBcufileops\fR, \fBcufilter\fR, \fBcuundo\fR, \fBtclutils::tuprovider\fR
